####
# ======================================================================
# fledge - Ship a Ruby app to people who don't have Ruby
# ======================================================================
#
# SYNOPSIS
#     fledge build [OPTIONS] [PATH]
#     fledge --help
#     fledge --version
#
# DESCRIPTION
#     Put a Ruby app, and a Ruby to run it, in one folder that runs on
#     a computer with no Ruby installed.
#
#     The Ruby on your computer can't travel. It was built for one
#     machine, with that machine's paths and libraries baked in, so a
#     copy breaks anywhere else. Fledge starts instead from a runtime:
#     a Ruby built to be moved, and a small launcher that runs your
#     app on it. Fledge copies your app into the runtime for each
#     platform you ask for, so you can build for macOS, Windows, and
#     Linux from one computer.
#
#     An app is a folder with a main.rb. Run it during development
#     with your own Ruby; build it to ship it.
#
# COMMANDS
#     build              Copy the app in PATH (default: the current
#                        directory) into a runtime for each target,
#                        then archive each result. Prints the path of
#                        each archive it writes, one per line.
#
# OPTIONS
#     -t, --target PLATFORM
#                        Build for PLATFORM (repeatable). One of:
#                        arm64-darwin, x86_64-linux, aarch64-linux,
#                        x64-mingw-ucrt (default: the platform you are
#                        on)
#     -o, --out DIR      Write builds to DIR (default: dist)
#     -h, --help         Show this help
#     -V, --version      Print Fledge's version
#
# FILES
#     PATH/main.rb       The file the launcher runs. The app's folder
#                        is on its load path, so require "foo" finds
#                        PATH/foo.rb. Everything in PATH ships except
#                        PATH/.git and DIR, if DIR is inside PATH.
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM/ The built app, ready to run where it lands.
#                        NAME is PATH's folder name, and NAME is also
#                        the program to run (NAME.exe on Windows). A
#                        new build replaces it.
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM.zip
#     DIR/NAME-PLATFORM.tar.gz
#                        The same folder, archived for download: a .zip
#                        for Windows, a .tar.gz for macOS and Linux.
#     ~/.cache/fledge/runtimes/
#                        Runtimes downloaded from Fledge's GitHub
#                        Release for this version, each checked
#                        against the release's SHA256SUMS.
#
# ENVIRONMENT
#     FLEDGE_RUNTIME_DIR Take runtimes from this folder of
#                        fledge-runtime-VERSION-PLATFORM.tar.gz files,
#                        and never download them.
#     XDG_CACHE_HOME     Keep downloaded runtimes in
#                        $XDG_CACHE_HOME/fledge/runtimes instead.
#
# EXIT STATUS
#     0                  Success
#     1                  Error (bad arguments, a missing app, or a
#                        failed build)
#
# EXAMPLES
#     Build the app in the current directory for this computer:
#     fledge build
#
#     Build for every platform, from any one of them:
#     fledge build -t arm64-darwin -t x86_64-linux -t aarch64-linux \
#       -t x64-mingw-ucrt
#
#     Build an app from elsewhere, into another folder:
#     fledge build --out /tmp/builds path/to/app
#
# ======================================================================
###
# Fledge is requested: an AI agent built it, with little or no review
# from a person. Its gates (tests, linters, and delivery to computers
# that have never seen Ruby) review it instead.

require "fledge/cli"
exit Fledge::CLI.new(help_path: __FILE__).call(ARGV)
